+# Narrowing NG-9: Filtering the Veterinarian Directory by Specialty
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+NG-9 declared that only owners are searchable — searching for a pet, a visit, or a veterinarian was out of scope, on the grounds that owner search demonstrates paged prefix search once and repeating it elsewhere adds surface rather than understanding.
+
+A request arrived to narrow the veterinarian directory to one specialty. The directory already displays the specialties each veterinarian holds (`REQ-VET-001`), so the narrowing reads a value the page already shows. NG-9 as written was broad enough to cover it. That forced the question: is this the capability NG-9 declined, or a different one?
+
+The request names both published forms of the directory — the browsable page and the machine-readable list. The machine-readable list was withdrawn on 2026-07-31 as `REQ-VET-002`, an implementation artifact with no consumer, marked for removal.
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Hold NG-9 as written** — decline the filter. The non-goal stays simple and the sample stays smaller.
+2. **Retire NG-9** — make every entity searchable, and drop the boundary the row records.
+3. **Narrow NG-9 to free-text search** — filtering a published directory on an attribute it already displays becomes in scope; free-text search over pets, visits, and veterinarians stays out.
+
+## Decision
+
+We take option 3. NG-9 now declines free-text veterinarian, pet, and visit search. Filtering the veterinarian directory by a specialty it already displays is in scope, and `REQ-VET-004` states it.
+
+The distinction is what a reader learns from the code. Owner search demonstrates paged prefix matching over typed text; repeating that pattern for a second entity teaches nothing new, which is the ground NG-9 protects. A whole-value, case-insensitive filter over a bounded reference list is a different pattern, and it is the one the request asks for.
+
+Option 1 declines the request on a ground NG-9 does not hold once the two patterns are separated. Option 2 discards the boundary the Non-Goals table exists to record.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- The filter applies to both published forms of the directory. The machine-readable list therefore acquires its first named capability and returns as a supported surface under `REQ-VET-003`. `REQ-VET-002` stays retired and its ID is never reused.
+- NG-9 now covers less ground, so its edge is easier to cross by accident. A later request to match part of a specialty name, or to find a veterinarian by name, falls under NG-9 and needs its own decision.
+- NG-2 is untouched. The directory stays read-only: filtering reads specialties, it does not manage them.
+- Two surfaces now carry the same filter rule. A change to matching semantics has to land on both or they diverge.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Non-goal:** NG-9
+
+## References
+
+- [prd.md#req-vet-003](../prd.md#req-vet-003) — the machine-readable veterinarian list as a supported surface
+- [prd.md#req-vet-004](../prd.md#req-vet-004) — narrowing the directory to one specialty
+- [system-design.md#contracts](../system-design.md#contracts)
